speechtext:
I was about to say that I do not claim to have a knowledge of the real benefits of this collection service such as the fishermen have, but I am beginning to think that my hon. friend has not that knowledge 'either. This petition was addressed to myself and to the hon. Minister of Fisheries, and I should like to know from my hon. friend whether or not it correctly represents the views of the fishermen. I have been under the impression that this collection service is of substantial benefit and I must confess that I have done all in my power to support it and assist in having it started, believing that it would be in the interests of the fishermen. The following petition was passed unanimously by the fishermen's union:
At a large and representative meeting of the fishermen's union of L'Ardoise district the following resolutions were unanimously adopted:
Whereas the fishermen of the L'Ardoise district have heretofore been greatly handicapped on account of lack of adequate transportation facilities;
And whereas the federal government has subsidized collection boats to collect fish from the various sections along the southern and eastern shores of Nova Scotia;
And whereas a collection boat can be successfully operated from the port of L'Ardoise at least during the spring and summer months, and thus enable the fishermen of that port to dispose of their catch on equal terms with those of other districts already served by collection boats;
And whereas LWrdoise is one of the largest and most important fishing centres in the province of Nova Scotia;
It is therefore resolved that the Department of Fisheries be respectfully requested to make provision for a fish collection boat to serve the district of L'Ardoise during the coming season.
It was resolved that a copy of the resolution be forwarded to the Hon. Mr. Cardin, Minister of Marine and Fisheries, and to myself. The petition was signed by the president, Mr. Wilfred Mombourquette, and by the secretary, Mr. A. P. Sampson, of the fishermen's union, and I took the petition at its face value. I believe that the gentlemen who signed it and the gentlemen whom they represented knew what they were talking about, and until my hon. friend spoke this afternoon I had thought that the collection service was considered to be in the best interests of the fishermen.
The only objections I have received to this service have been from representatives of the companies. One company did not give any particular reason, but stated that they did not favour the proposition. Another company wrote to me;
We suppose that such a move is intended to raise the prices to the fishermen, but we, as curers cannot stand any increase.
[The Chairman.1
Even though these objections were received the collection service was put into effect. My hon. friend says that he has no desire to play politics in this matter; if that is so, I think he should give credit where the credit is due, and I was surprised to hear him criticize this service this afternoon and again this evening. I should like to know whether he is for or against this collection service. The service was inaugurated in my own constituency and as a result the fishermen were enabled to fish as late as the middle of January whereas previously they had been forced to stop fishing about the end of October. It was regarded as a substantial benefit. In that connection I should like to read an extract from a lettev which I received, as follows:
The boat is a great help to all concerned and everyone is anxious to make a success of it so *that the service could be continued another year. It surely makes a great difference to all concerned in the fishing industry in this locality.
That letter was written early this year. The petition which I have read was signed in February 1929. I think %ve should deal with this matter fairly. These measures were thought to be of great assistance to the fishing industry, particularly in the outlying places and the fishermen are approving them and asking for more. If my hon. friend is against the service I should like him to say so.
